1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Coach: Checking your understanding means making your interpretation available for the other person to confirm

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,857
Coach: or correct.

3
00:00:06,857 --> 00:00:11,143
Coach: It is useful when the meaning matters to your response,

4
00:00:11,143 --> 00:00:13,714
Coach: not a ritual before every turn.

5
00:00:14,064 --> 00:00:19,207
Coach: The group is still planning a thirty-minute beginner paper-folding workshop,

6
00:00:19,207 --> 00:00:21,778
Coach: with one facilitator and shared materials.

7
00:00:21,778 --> 00:00:25,635
Coach: Participants should have opportunities to try and ask questions.

8
00:00:25,635 --> 00:00:29,064
Coach: The plan has not been adopted or tested.

9
00:00:29,414 --> 00:00:34,557
Noah: I would keep the demonstration brief, so people have time to try.

10
00:00:34,557 --> 00:00:40,128
Noah: I am worried that someone who misses a step could get left behind.

11
00:00:40,478 --> 00:00:45,621
Coach: Suppose you want to check this whole contribution before proposing a change.

12
00:00:45,621 --> 00:00:49,050
Coach: Keep the brief demonstration, the reason for it,

13
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:51,621
Coach: and the possible difficulty in view.

14
00:00:51,971 --> 00:00:54,543
Coach: A paraphrase can use different words.

15
00:00:54,543 --> 00:01:00,114
Coach: What matters is whether the listener can recognise their point, including its limits.

16
00:01:00,114 --> 00:01:04,400
Coach: Changing could to will makes a possible difficulty sound certain.

17
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:07,750
Coach: Leaving something out is a different issue.

18
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:12,893
Coach: A short check may be useful when only one detail needs confirming.

19
00:01:12,893 --> 00:01:17,607
Coach: Here, however, we are checking both the purpose and the concern.

20
00:01:17,957 --> 00:01:21,385
Coach: Before choosing a response, recall the original point.

21
00:01:21,385 --> 00:01:26,528
Coach: What does Noah want to keep, why does he want it brief,

22
00:01:26,528 --> 00:01:29,528
Coach: and what does he say could happen?

23
00:01:37,878 --> 00:01:41,307
Coach: Each response uses part of the same topic.

24
00:01:41,307 --> 00:01:44,307
Coach: Compare what it preserves, what it omits,

25
00:01:44,307 --> 00:01:47,735
Coach: and whether it makes the original claim stronger.

26
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:51,164
Coach: Choose the most faithful check for this moment.

27
00:01:51,514 --> 00:01:57,086
Coach: The replies that follow are authored examples of how this exchange could continue.

28
00:01:57,086 --> 00:02:02,657
Coach: They illustrate the wording; they do not predict how every speaker would react.

29
00:02:11,007 --> 00:02:15,721
You: You want a brief demonstration so people have time to try,

30
00:02:15,721 --> 00:02:21,293
You: and you are concerned that someone who misses a step might fall behind.

31
00:02:21,643 --> 00:02:22,072
Noah: Yes.

32
00:02:22,072 --> 00:02:25,072
Noah: I want time for people to try,

33
00:02:25,072 --> 00:02:31,072
Noah: while keeping a demonstration and a way to help if someone misses a step.

34
00:02:31,422 --> 00:02:36,136
Coach: This paraphrase keeps the proposal, its purpose and the possible difficulty.

35
00:02:36,136 --> 00:02:39,136
Coach: Noah confirms that reading in this example.

36
00:02:39,136 --> 00:02:40,850
Coach: The wording is different,

37
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:45,136
Coach: but the uncertainty and the connection between the parts remain.

38
00:02:45,486 --> 00:02:49,772
Coach: A useful check preserves the point you need to understand.

39
00:02:49,772 --> 00:02:54,486
Coach: In this exchange, that includes a brief demonstration, time to try,

40
00:02:54,486 --> 00:02:57,486
Coach: and the possibility of missing a step.

41
00:02:57,486 --> 00:02:59,200
Coach: None cancels the others.

42
00:02:59,550 --> 00:03:03,836
Coach: When the meaning is already clear, you can respond directly.

43
00:03:03,836 --> 00:03:07,693
Coach: When a small detail is uncertain, check that detail.

44
00:03:07,693 --> 00:03:11,979
Coach: Repeating an entire contribution may use time without improving understanding.

45
00:03:12,329 --> 00:03:15,758
Coach: A check should also leave room for correction.

46
00:03:15,758 --> 00:03:20,901
Coach: You are offering your reading, not telling someone what they must mean.

47
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:25,615
Coach: Their reply may confirm it, narrow it or change your understanding.

48
00:03:25,965 --> 00:03:30,679
Coach: Try a brief check of Noah’s meaning in your own words.

49
00:03:30,679 --> 00:03:36,251
Coach: Keep the proposal and its purpose, and preserve the possibility in his concern.

50
00:03:36,251 --> 00:03:39,679
Coach: You do not need to copy the example.

51
00:03:40,029 --> 00:03:43,029
Coach: You can think first or speak privately.

52
00:03:43,029 --> 00:03:45,172
Coach: Nothing is recorded or assessed.

53
00:03:45,172 --> 00:03:51,172
Coach: There is a short pause here; pause playback yourself if you want more time.

54
00:03:59,522 --> 00:04:04,236
Coach: If you tried a version, compare its meaning with the source.

55
00:04:04,236 --> 00:04:08,950
Coach: Did you add certainty, lose the purpose, or leave something unclear?

56
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:13,236
Coach: If you did not rehearse, you can return when ready.

57
00:04:13,586 --> 00:04:17,443
Coach: Paraphrasing helps when you have a reading to check.

58
00:04:17,443 --> 00:04:21,300
Coach: Sometimes the words themselves leave the relevant part unspecified.

59
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:26,443
Coach: Then a focused question may be more useful than a longer summary.

60
00:04:26,793 --> 00:04:29,364
Coach: Next we will examine that choice:

61
00:04:29,364 --> 00:04:32,364
Coach: asking for the information needed to respond,

62
00:04:32,364 --> 00:04:34,936
Coach: while leaving already clear details alone.

63
00:04:34,936 --> 00:04:39,650
Coach: Keep the question connected to the discussion in front of you.
